{"name":"Sardines / oily fish","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/School_of_sardines_at_the_Monterey_Bay_Aquarium_%2812056%29.jpg/960px-School_of_sardines_at_the_Monterey_Bay_Aquarium_%2812056%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:School_of_sardines_at_the_Monterey_Bay_Aquarium_(12056).jpg","caption":"School of sardines at the Monterey Bay Aquarium (12056)","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["Sardines are small oily fish eaten fresh, grilled, smoked or canned, valued as an inexpensive source of protein, B12 and omega-3 fats. Some in appearance communities eat them for healthier-looking skin.","The route people point to is omega-3 intake lowering skin inflammation. Canned Pacific sardines provide 375 percent of the daily value of B12 per 100 grams and calcium when eaten with the bones. No clinical studies on sardines for skin were found in the sources reviewed."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People eat canned sardines on toast, with rice or in salads, a few times a week as a budget protein. Canned fish is shelf-stable, which makes it easy to keep up."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Sardines are described as lower in mercury than many fish, and canned versions contain notable sodium. Pacific sardine fisheries have faced closures, which can affect supply."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/School_of_sardines_at_the_Monterey_Bay_Aquarium_%2812056%29.jpg/960px-School_of_sardines_at_the_Monterey_Bay_Aquarium_%2812056%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/School_of_sardines_at_the_Monterey_Bay_Aquarium_%2812056%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"S