 the sources reviewed."},"wikipedia":null,"grokipedia":null,"lead":["Sardines are small, oily forage fish eaten fresh, grilled, smoked or canned. In appearance-focused diet discussion they are promoted as a source of omega-3 fatty acids said to benefit skin [1].","Canned Pacific sardines are notable for protein and vitamin B12, with 375 percent of the daily value per 100 grams, and provide calcium when eaten with bones [1]. The reference article gives no omega-3 quantities and cites no clinical studies on skin [1]."],"infobox":[["Type","Food (small oily fish)"],["Protein","About 21 g per 100 g (canned)"],["Vitamin B12","375% DV per 100 g (canned)"],["Calcium","18% DV per 100 g (canned, with bone)"],["Evidence for skin use","Not examined in the sources reviewed"]],"sections":[{"h":"Nutrition","paras":["Per 100 grams, canned Pacific sardines provide about 185 kilocalories, 20.9 grams of protein and 10.4 grams of fat, plus B12, phosphorus, niacin, riboflavin, calcium, iron and zinc [1]."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Contaminants and sustainability","paras":["Because they sit low on the food chain, sardines are described as carrying less mercury than many other commonly eaten fish [1]. In April 2015 the Pacific Fishery Management Council halted the US West Coast commercial sardine season after stocks collapsed [1]. The IUCN lists the European pilchard as Least Concern [1]."],"subs":[]},{"h":"History","paras":["Cornish pilchard fishing thrived from about 1750 to 1880, and the last large US sardine cannery closed in 2010 [1]."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["No studies found on sardines specifically for skin or appearance outcomes."],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,"timeline":[{"year":"1750","event":"Cornish pilchard industry begins its main period [1]."},{"year":"2010","event":"Last large US sardine cannery closes [1]."},{"year":"2015","event":"US West Coast commercial sardine fishing halted [1]."}],"refs":[{"title":"Sardine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sardine","sou